# Simple OS – protection and maintenance in the background (settings: hook 12_system_services, Control Center)
# Firewall: incoming all closed, outgoing open; printer discovery (mDNS) and UPnP are allowed by ufw's default rules
ufw
# AppArmor (#48): Debian's kernel has the security module on, but without these tools no profile was ever loaded.
# It loads the profiles packages bring themselves (CUPS, man, dhclient, LibreOffice – the latter in complain mode,
# i.e. only logged). Tested in 1.2 with printing (cupsd + cups-pdf enforced), LibreOffice, Firefox, Flatpak apps
# (Bottles, VSCodium), the keyring: nothing blocked except cupsd's harmless "net_admin" at start. No extra profile
# sets (apparmor-profiles) on purpose: they would confine apps we never tested with them. A one-shot at boot, no daemon.
apparmor
# When memory is full, end the largest program instead of the computer freezing for minutes
earlyoom
# Power profiles (Power saver / Balanced / Performance, Control Center → Power); thermald keeps Intel CPUs cool
power-profiles-daemon
thermald
# BIOS/firmware updates from LVFS (Lenovo, Dell, HP …) via Software Updates; signed EFI part for Secure Boot
fwupd
fwupd-amd64-signed
# initramfs-tools packs with zstd (COMPRESS=zstd in initramfs.conf) – without the program it fell back to gzip
# ("W: No zstd … using gzip"; only recommended, and the image is built without Recommends): slower to unpack at boot
zstd
